{"name":"Queer Blue","hex":"#88ace0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"queer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Homosexual. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Non-heterosexual or non-cisgender: homosexual, bisexual, asexual, transgender, etc."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who is or appears homosexual, or who has homosexual qualities.","A person of any non-heterosexual sexuality or sexual identity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/queer"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Queer","blocks":[{"text":"Queer is an umbrella term for sexual and gender minorities. It is alternately used to refer to people who reject sexual and gender norms and share radical politics characterized by solidarity across lines of identity. Queer is also a self-identity term for many people, characterized by rejection or disruption of binary categories of sexual orientation and gender."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Gender_symbols_%284_colors%29.svg/330px-Gender_symbols_%284_colors%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queer","wikidata":"Q51415"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg/500px-Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg/1280px-Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg/500px-Drag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drag Ball in Webster Hall, c. 1920s. Many queer-identifying men distanced themselves from the \"flagrant\" public image of gay men as effeminate \"fairies\".[29]: 16, 298 ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrag_Ball_in_Webster_Hall--1920s.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/%22Queer_Resistsance_Against_the_Cuts%22.jpg/500px-%22Queer_Resistsance_Against_the_Cuts%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/%22Queer_Resistsance_Against_the_Cuts%22.jpg/1280px-%22Queer_Resistsance_Against_the_Cuts%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thu